DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     Epochs are used to guarantee liveness and immutability of data by defer‐
     ring reclamation and mutation until a grace period has elapsed.  Epochs
     do not have any lock ordering issues.  Entering and leaving an epoch sec‐
     tion will never block.

     Epochs are allocated with eeppoocchh__aalllloocc().  The _n_a_m_e argument is used for
     debugging convenience when the EEPPOOCCHH__TTRRAACCEE kernel option is configured.
     By default, epochs do not allow preemption during sections.  By default
     mutexes cannot be held across eeppoocchh__wwaaiitt__pprreeeemmpptt().  The _f_l_a_g_s specified
     are formed by _O_R'ing the following values:

           EPOCH_LOCKED
                   Permit holding mutexes across eeppoocchh__wwaaiitt__pprreeeemmpptt() (re‐
                   quires EPOCH_PREEMPT).  When doing this one must be cau‐
                   tious of creating a situation where a deadlock is possible.

           EPOCH_PREEMPT
                   The _e_p_o_c_h will allow preemption during sections.  Only non-
                   sleepable locks may be acquired during a preemptible epoch.
                   The functions eeppoocchh__eenntteerr__pprreeeemmpptt(), eeppoocchh__eexxiitt__pprreeeemmpptt(),
                   and eeppoocchh__wwaaiitt__pprreeeemmpptt() must be used in place of
                   eeppoocchh__eenntteerr(), eeppoocchh__eexxiitt(), and eeppoocchh__wwaaiitt(), respec‐
                   tively.

     _e_p_o_c_hs are freed with eeppoocchh__ffrreeee().

     Threads indicate the start of an epoch critical section by calling
     eeppoocchh__eenntteerr() (or eeppoocchh__eenntteerr__pprreeeemmpptt() for preemptible epochs).  Threads
     call eeppoocchh__eexxiitt() (or eeppoocchh__eexxiitt__pprreeeemmpptt() for preemptible epochs) to in‐
     dicate the end of a critical section.  _s_t_r_u_c_t _e_p_o_c_h___t_r_a_c_k_e_rs are stack
     objects whose pointers are passed to eeppoocchh__eenntteerr__pprreeeemmpptt() and
     eeppoocchh__eexxiitt__pprreeeemmpptt() (much like _s_t_r_u_c_t _r_m___p_r_i_o_t_r_a_c_k_e_r).

     Threads can defer work until a grace period has expired since any thread
     has entered the epoch either synchronously or asynchronously.
     eeppoocchh__ccaallll() defers work asynchronously by invoking the provided _c_a_l_l_b_a_c_k
     at a later time.  eeppoocchh__wwaaiitt() (or eeppoocchh__wwaaiitt__pprreeeemmpptt()) blocks the cur‐
     rent thread until the grace period has expired and the work can be done
     safely.

     Default, non-preemptible epoch wait (eeppoocchh__wwaaiitt()) is guaranteed to have
     much shorter completion times relative to preemptible epoch wait
     (eeppoocchh__wwaaiitt__pprreeeemmpptt()).  (In the default type, none of the threads in an
     epoch section will be preempted before completing its section.)

     INVARIANTS can assert that a thread is in an epoch by using iinn__eeppoocchh().
     iinn__eeppoocchh(_e_p_o_c_h) is equivalent to invoking iinn__eeppoocchh__vveerrbboossee(_e_p_o_c_h, _0).  If
     EEPPOOCCHH__TTRRAACCEE is enabled, iinn__eeppoocchh__vveerrbboossee(_e_p_o_c_h, _1) provides additional
     verbose debugging information.

     The epoch API currently does not support sleeping in epoch_preempt sec‐
     tions.  A caller should never call eeppoocchh__wwaaiitt() in the middle of an epoch
     section for the same epoch as this will lead to a deadlock.

     The eeppoocchh__ddrraaiinn__ccaallllbbaacckkss() function is used to drain all pending call‐
     backs which have been invoked by prior eeppoocchh__ccaallll() function calls on the
     same epoch.  This function is useful when there are shared memory struc‐
     ture(s) referred to by the epoch callback(s) which are not refcounted and
     are rarely freed.  The typical place for calling this function is right
     before freeing or invalidating the shared resource(s) used by the epoch
     callback(s).  This function can sleep and is not optimized for perfor‐
     mance.

